\description{
Tests whether the taxa in each sample are more closely related
(phylogenetic clustering) or more distantly related (overdispersion)
than in random communities: the standardized effect sizes of \code{\link[=mpd]{mpd()}}
and \code{\link[=mntd]{mntd()}} against a null model. Their negatives are Webb's Net
Relatedness Index (\strong{NRI}, from MPD) and Nearest Taxon Index (\strong{NTI},
from MNTD), so positive values mean clustering. This is picante's
\code{ses.mpd()} and \code{ses.mntd()}, and phylocom's \code{comstruct}.
}
\details{
\strong{Null models}\tabular{lll}{
   \code{null_model} \tab Each random community \tab Keeps \cr
   \code{"taxa_labels"} \tab the tips' labels shuffled: the table's taxa are moved to tips drawn at random from \strong{every} tip of \code{tree} (or label of \code{taxa_dm}) \tab the table \cr
   \code{"richness"} \tab each sample's values moved to taxa drawn at random from all the table's taxa \tab each sample's richness and values \cr
   \code{"frequency"} \tab each taxon's values moved to samples drawn at random \tab each taxon's frequency and values \cr
   \code{"sample_pool"} \tab as \code{"richness"}, from the taxa present in at least one sample \tab richness \cr
   \code{"phylogeny_pool"} \tab as \code{"richness"}, from every tip of \code{tree} (or label of \code{taxa_dm}) \tab richness \cr
   \code{"independent_swap"} \tab \code{n_swaps} swaps of 2 \eqn{\times} 2 checkerboards (Gotelli 2000) \tab each sample's richness and each taxon's frequency \cr
   \code{"trial_swap"} \tab \code{n_swaps} attempted swaps (Miklós & Podani 2004) \tab as above \cr
   \code{"exact"} \tab (no draws) the exact mean and standard deviation of MPD over random sets of tips, as \code{"phylogeny_pool"} \tab richness \cr
}


A sample's abundances go with its taxa: under the pool models and
\code{"richness"} they are given in random order to the drawn taxa, and in
the swaps each taxon keeps its values (as in picante and phylocom). They
matter only with \code{weighted = TRUE}.

Which null model answers which question is discussed by Kembel (2009)
and Hardy (2008): \code{"taxa_labels"} and \code{"phylogeny_pool"} ask whether the
sample is a random draw from the tree, \code{"richness"} and \code{"sample_pool"}
whether it is a random draw from the taxa observed, and the swaps also
hold how common each taxon is. Because \code{"taxa_labels"} and
\code{"phylogeny_pool"} draw from \strong{every tip}, a tree with many taxa that
could never occur in the study makes every sample look clustered: prune
the tree to the study's species pool first (picante gives the same
advice).

\strong{Exact moments} (\code{null_model = "exact"}, \code{mpd_ses()} only). For
\eqn{S} tips drawn at random from all \eqn{T} without replacement (the
\code{"phylogeny_pool"} model; per sample, also \code{"taxa_labels"}), with the
sample's abundances given to them at random, MPD is a sum over pairs of
positions. Its mean is the mean distance \eqn{\mu} between two distinct
tips, and its variance follows from three sums over the tips: of the
squared deviations \eqn{(d_{ab} - \mu)^2}, and of the squares of each
tip's total deviation \eqn{\sum_b (d_{ab} - \mu)}, weighted by how many
pairs of sample positions share two, one or no positions. With a tree,
all three take one pass over its branches, so no draws are needed (as
in Tsirogiannis, Sandel & Cheliotis 2012, for the unweighted MPD). Then
\code{z} is exact, but has no rank or p-value; \code{pnorm(z)} is a normal
approximation to the lower-tail p-value. No exact form is implemented
for MNTD.

\strong{Speed and memory}

Each random community's MPD (MNTD) is computed from the tree as in
\code{\link[=mpd]{mpd()}}, in time proportional to \eqn{S \log S}, with no taxa
\eqn{\times} taxa matrix. Draws run in parallel, each from its own
random-number stream, and are summed in a fixed order, so results are
identical for any number of \code{cpus}. The swap models keep one samples
\eqn{\times} taxa map per thread (4 bytes per cell), and use fewer
threads when the maps would take more than 1 GB.

\strong{Differences from picante}

picante 1.8.4's \code{ses.mpd()} and \code{ses.mntd()} draw with R's random
numbers, ecodive with its own (PCG), so the null values differ draw by
draw; given the same random communities, the results agree to about
\eqn{10^{-12}}. Other differences:
\itemize{
\item picante's \code{"sample.pool"} makes the same communities as its
\code{"richness"}, drawing from every column of the table, including taxa
absent from every sample; ecodive's \code{"sample_pool"} draws only from
taxa present in some sample. They agree when no taxon is absent from
every sample.
\item picante's shuffles for \code{"richness"} and \code{"frequency"} are not quite
uniform (each position's chance of holding a value varies by a few
percent); ecodive's are.
\item With \code{"frequency"}, a random sample can have fewer than two taxa.
picante leaves those draws out of the mean and standard deviation but
still divides the rank by \code{runs + 1}; ecodive divides by the number of
draws used plus one.
\item When a sample has fewer than two taxa, picante gives \code{NaN} for
\code{mpd.rand.mean} (all draws \code{NA}) and an arbitrary rank and p-value if
it isn't \code{NA}; ecodive gives \code{NA}.
\item Random values within \eqn{10^{-12}} (relative) of \code{obs} count as ties.
\item Each swap-model draw is a chain of its own from the observed table, as
in picante (phylocom instead continues one chain). When no swap is
possible (every pair of samples nested), picante's independent swap
never ends; ecodive warns and uses the observed table.
}

\strong{Same names in other packages}

picante calls these \code{ses.mpd()} and \code{ses.mntd()}; there are no
functions named \code{nri()} or \code{nti()}.
}
\section{Input Types}{


The \code{counts} parameter is designed to accept a simple numeric matrix, but
seamlessly supports objects from the following biological data packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{phyloseq}
\item \code{rbiom}
\item \code{SummarizedExperiment}
\item \code{TreeSummarizedExperiment}
}

For large datasets, standard matrix operations may be slow. See
\code{vignette('performance')} for details on using optimized formats
(e.g. sparse matrices) and parallel processing.
}

